{"skill":{"slug":"x-api","displayName":"X Api"},"version":{"version":"0.1.0","createdAt":1769635911554,"changelog":"- Initial release of x-api: post tweets to X (Twitter) using the official API with OAuth 1.0a.\n- Bypasses rate limits and bot detection—more reliable than cookie-based CLI tools.\n- Clear setup instructions for obtaining and configuring API credentials.\n- Supports multi-line tweet posts and returns the tweet URL on success.\n- Specifies API usage limits, troubleshooting steps, and distinguishes use cases vs. the bird CLI for reading.","changelogSource":"auto","license":null,"files":[{"path":"README.md","size":2325,"sha256":"5bb31762750d2064d0f192813ba4c4875edc669cc462383c32742fe441b46299","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"SKILL.md","size":2399,"sha256":"0f5643478398d78c8c863f881cadbea3ca70a1e4dba5862cff955e49c8a96162","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/package.json","size":129,"sha256":"fcf0ed74c5572e7da73009edb4a173aa6ef0177208791b91a9eef566fd35768f","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"scripts/x-post.mjs","size":2289,"sha256":"e7f92b07490bb7c79ffd63846e3d93495b11cbfb4d46433a5e9587798602a94d","contentType":"text/plain"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2086,"sha256":"3aa2fb0f60e58bbff0fea16cb02445e9ec9fe272583a095b9d4a210c2b776586","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"clean","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789023059496,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"ae30e21c83966a4cc93351a6c5950c78933889fb9807d4f7fbd60c5595f2be1f","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/ae30e21c83966a4cc93351a6c5950c78933889fb9807d4f7fbd60c5595f2be1f","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: x-api\nVersion: 0.1.0\n\nThe OpenClaw AgentSkills skill bundle for 'x-api' is benign. Its stated purpose is to post to X (Twitter) using the official API. The `SKILL.md` and `scripts/x-post.mjs` clearly outline the need for X API credentials, which are loaded from environment variables or a designated secrets file (`~/.clawdbot/secrets/x-api.json`). The core script uses the legitimate `twitter-api-v2` library to interact with the X API. There is no evidence of data exfiltration beyond the intended API calls, malicious execution, persistence mechanisms, prompt injection attempts against the agent, or suspicious dependencies.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779160247675},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":77,"severity":"HIGH","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":13,"checkedAt":1789023059496},"llm":{"status":"clean","verdict":"benign","normalizedStatus":"clean","confidence":"high","summary":"This is a straightforward X posting skill, but users should understand it uses write-capable X credentials and a live posting command.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Credential access is limited to named X environment variables and documented X-specific config files, which is proportionate for OAuth API posting.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The package uses an unpinned npm dependency and the skill documentation suggests a global npm install, which creates avoidable supply-chain and scope risk.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The posting behavior is disclosed, but the examples do not include a confirmation or dry-run step before publishing to a real X account.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The skill asks users to store credentials and install the skill under the Clawdbot directory, but it does not create cron jobs, services, startup hooks, or other hidden persistence.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"note"},{"detail":"The documented purpose is to post to X using the official API, and the script does exactly that with user-supplied text.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"ok"}],"guidance":"Install only if you are comfortable giving this skill write access to an X account. Use a dedicated or low-risk X app/token, review posts before running the command, avoid global npm installation, and prefer pinned dependencies with a lockfile.","findings":"[PE3] expected: The references to API keys and access tokens are setup instructions and OAuth credentials required for posting; the code does not show unrelated credential harvesting or exfiltration.\n[RA2] expected: The cited ~/.clawdbot paths are normal skill installation and optional credential-storage paths, not unauthorized background persistence.\n[SQP-2] expected: The command does immediately publish content, which is the skill's stated purpose; lack of a confirmation prompt is a usability and safety note rather than hidden behavior.\n[LP3] expected: The skill does not declare a richer permission boundary, but its artifact text clearly documents the specific X credential sources it uses.\n[SC1] unexpected: The caret dependency range and missing lockfile are real supply-chain weaknesses and should be fixed, but they are not evidence that the current artifact is malicious.\n[SDI-2] expected: Filesystem credential loading is disclosed in both the skill instructions and script comments, and it is limited to X-specific credential files.\n[T08] unexpected: The static scan's unpinned-dependency finding is valid as a supply-chain risk, especially with write-capable X credentials, but it does not override the coherent and disclosed core behavior.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789023059496}}}}}